many economists breathed a sigh of  relief after march's consumer price  index was released yes the report showed  the annual u.s inflation rate was the  highest since 1981 but  it also suggested inflation may have  peaked and will begin to slow down since  then however markets have shown  investors expect higher inflation for  longer i'll explain why longer-term  inflation expectations have picked up  what we're seeing in the market that  shows this and what that means for the  broader economy let's get into it i  think we're at peak inflation  and because of that we're a peak fear  investors don't think inflation's going  to keep rising but they also don't think  it's going to come down very quickly  that's due to some new uncertainties in  the world and some old ones being  exacerbated the first is the war in  ukraine which has continued longer than  most expected and currently has no end  in sight that's keeping pressure on oil  and wheat prices which are major inputs  to a slew of other food and household  products there is also the recent covett  outbreak in china that has led to  lockdowns and further disrupted supply  chains and housing prices are still  rising at a double-digit pace in the u.s  outstripping economists expectations  when investors expect high inflation  bond yields rise and prices fall so we  can see this in markets recently we've  seen yields on longer dated government  bonds like 10 20 and 30 year maturities  reaching multi-year highs the 10-year  break-even rate which measures investors  inflation expectations over a decade  touched a record high in april rising  above 3 percent for the first time since  tracking began in 2003. i asked rick  reeder managing director at blackrock  about the market moves he said that  inflation has gotten stickier meaning it  will be harder to get rid of he agrees  that inflation is probably peaking but  warns it's not like you're rapidly going  down the other side of a mountain it's  more like you've climbed the mountain  and now it's going to be a gentle  descent down from these levels of  inflation stickier inflation means  prices continue to rise which will add  more pressure to family budgets it'll  also put things like home ownership out  of reach for more people but a slower  yet still elevated pace of inflation  could mean that wages start to keep pace  with overall price increases so average  americans could start to see real pay  increases for the first time in quite a  while higher inflation also creates an  environment where there are more assets  to invest in that offer some income with  less risk that's especially good for  retirees and those on a fixed budget but  any positive outcome for the economy  hinges on the federal reserve being able  to rein in inflation to be sure runaway  inflation has never happened in the u.s  and few think it's likely now but  runaway inflation erodes the value of a  country's currency hobbles the economy  and makes even basic household goods  difficult to afford that's why central  banks prefer to inflict a little pain by  raising interest rates to slow down the  economy then let inflation get out of  control the fed has made clear recently  that they think there's a lot more work  to be done they're expected to raise u.s  interest rates at twice the normal pace  at their next meeting and the fed fund  futures market shows investors believe  they'll raise rates double time at  multiple meetings this year subhadra  rajapa head of u.s rate strategy at  cecilte general tells me that inflation  expectations are also rising because the  market is starting to question the fed's  ability to be effective at this point  the inflation battle has economic  organizations like the world bank imf  and oecd all lowering their expectations  for global growth and increasing their  expectations for inflation so buckle up  because this could be a bumpy ride  andre 3000 of the legendary outcast had  some wise advice for times like this he  said promise me you go stack promise me  you gonna ball promise me you'll invest  three-fourths of it all  that's all for now see you next time
 

